r the better  um  you know I think there's a you know five  years later I think there's  there's a handful of things that have  changed I think from a from an industry  standpoint  um there's a little bit  I think there's more emphasis to have  the uncomfortable conversations  um being the entertainment capital of  the world we attract a lot we attract  big events and  When We Gather in a room with ourselves  with stakeholders and Community Partners  um you're now in a room where we can  have those difficult conversations  nobody wants to talk about what happens  if there's a bomb what happens if  there's this device what happens if  somebody decides to to do something bad  and some of them are I think easier to  talk about as opposed to others  I think there's this underlying  I don't think I know you know in our  world in that first responder world  there's lots of conversation about  what's going to happen next  not if it's going to happen but when is  it going to happen and so I think  there's a you know there's a more  emphasis from from the industry from  from what we're doing to ensure that the  safety of all the of all those that live  here visit here and go to these events  because  at the end of the day  um  evil is not going to Prevail five years  later evil has not prevailed there's  been some challenging things that have  happened in our communities some bad  things that have happened in our  community but I think five years later  we're still  we're still this tight-knit community  we're still here to look out for one  another  we're still we're still Vegas strong  personally what's changed  you know up until up until a week ago it  was it's  still trying to wrap my my head around  that it's been five years  it's you know it's  it's  difficult  it's you know lots of reflection you  know that night unfortunately I lost a  friend who's amongst the one of the 58  at that passed  and and those thoughts come back  this year was the first year that  I went back through text messages that I  had from five years ago everything from  people that were checking in on me to  the text that I sent to to my family  to the text that I sent to to somebody  that died  and that was the first time in in the  five years I'm sure I looked at them you  know post post the event but probably  the first time that kind of stirred  these emotions at  you know  in those  I think we have a tendency to  compartmentalize where we put feelings  and where our emotions are stored and  for me five years later it kind of it's  opened up some of those but for me it  again it reminds me of the community  that I'm a part of it reminds me of the  opportunity I have to be able to do what  I do to be able to sit down and be able  to share  my story to be able to share the story  of the hundreds and thousands of people  in our community that helped that night  for those that are complete strangers to  our community that every year they come  back  um and want to  they're part of this unique Community  that's that's been created after such a  horrific event  I mean our communities  stood up for the  the minutes and the hours and the days  and the weeks and the years since that  event there was a  there was a it was a it was a different  and it still is there was almost a turn  um you know for for somebody you know  walking into a Starbucks in uniform  somebody holding the door open for you  and just saying thank you  um you know it's it's uh it's easy for  me to thank people  um but when people were thinking me it  was  maybe to this day it's hard  um you know and I don't I don't stand  behind the  um what's what you're paid to do it's  it's what you signed up for  nobody that wears our uniform signed up  for that night nobody that wears our  uniform signs up to get shot at nobody  signs up for this career to to deal with  demons and have nightmares is it a part  of the job it's become part of the job  but  um yeah the community has really showed  up that night and you know it's I think  it's just important that  we have these difficult conversations  um you know stop the bleed training  where we teach people how to put  tourniquets on and how you can  truly save somebody's life those are  tools that came out of bad experiences  you know we learn a lot of this stuff  because of war and that's a tool that  you know we used in EMS and it was a  tool that that night was an important  tool and you know a very Vivid thought  that night was seeing folks that would  take blankets that would take  stethoscopes that were taking seat belts  and turning them into tourniquets  because it's such an important tool and  uh yeah I mean I you know our I truly  believe that we're all placed where we  are for a reason  um we have bad things that happen in  life we have a few bad sour apples in  our in our society but we're a community  that is here to look out for one another  and support one another and anything we  can do to to make ourselves better in  turn makes our community that much  stronger
 

